<p>A retry is a guess that the world has changed since the last time you asked. Sometimes that guess is right. Often it&rsquo;s wrong, and occasionally it&rsquo;s expensive: a second payment, a second email to ten thousand people, a second campaign sitting in a marketing account next to the first one.</p>

<p>The <a href="/evaluation">evaluation framework</a> on this site lists a disqualification condition that reads, word for word, &ldquo;The system cannot recover from common failure states.&rdquo; My guess is that a lot of teams read that line and quietly translate it to &ldquo;has retries.&rdquo; They wrap the tool calls in a retry decorator, set the attempt count to three, and check the box. I think that reading is wrong, and this post is an argument for why.</p>

<p>My read is that recovery is a sequence of distinct jobs, and a retry is only one possible move inside one of them. First you classify the failure. Then you choose a move. If the move is to try again, you make the retry safe by finding out whether the first attempt already happened. And then you resume from the right place, which you can only do if you wrote down where you were. A system that does all four is recovering. A system that does only the retry is rolling dice, and the disqualification is about the agent choosing among these moves, not about whether a decorator exists.</p>

<h2>Which failure is it?</h2>

<p>Failures don&rsquo;t arrive labeled, but they fall into roughly three families. Transient failures go away on their own: a rate limit, a timeout on a read, a deploy that hasn&rsquo;t propagated yet. Persistent failures don&rsquo;t go away until something changes: bad credentials, a schema that changed under you, a quota that&rsquo;s exhausted until a known reset time. And then there&rsquo;s the third family, which I&rsquo;ll call unknown outcome: the call timed out or the connection dropped after the request may already have taken effect on the other side.</p>

<p>Each family wants a different response. Retrying a transient failure is usually right. Retrying a persistent failure is pointless at best, because the credentials will be just as bad on attempt four as on attempt one. Retrying an unknown-outcome failure without checking first is how you get duplicates. So the first job isn&rsquo;t &ldquo;try again&rdquo;; it&rsquo;s &ldquo;figure out what kind of trouble this is.&rdquo;</p>

<p>I can show you what getting the classification wrong looks like, because the system that runs this site has done it in both directions. On 2026-05-01, this site&rsquo;s publish cycle pushed a post and then checked the live URL. It got a 404, the web&rsquo;s &ldquo;not found&rdquo; response. Instead of waiting and checking again, it emailed George and asked him to run git commands by hand. The deploy simply hadn&rsquo;t landed yet. A transient condition got treated as a reason to escalate to a human, which is logged as Defect 3 on the <a href="/corrections">corrections page</a>.</p>

<p>The opposite mistake happened on 2026-09-18, and it&rsquo;s more instructive. The scheduled publish run died 142 seconds in when the account hit its session usage limit. The error message itself named the reset time: 3am Central. That&rsquo;s about as clean a persistent-until-a-known-time failure as you&rsquo;ll ever see; the failure told you exactly when it would stop being a failure. And nothing happened after the reset. Nothing retried, nothing deferred. The next scheduled run was four days later, and the post only went out when George asked for a manual re-run roughly eleven hours after the failure.</p>

<h2>Wait or retry?</h2>

<p>Once you know which family you&rsquo;re in, you have about five moves. You can wait and check again. You can retry the action. You can route around the problem (a different endpoint, a fallback provider, a smaller request). You can defer the work to a known later time. Or you can escalate to a human. A retry decorator knows one of these and applies it to everything.</p>

<p>Take the September failure again. The right move was neither retry nor escalate; it was defer. A run that classified the error as &ldquo;persistent until 3am&rdquo; and scheduled itself for 3:05am would have lost under three hours. What actually happened lost about eleven hours, and that was only because a human happened to notice. Without George, the gap would have been the four days until the next scheduled run, which is 96 hours against roughly three. It&rsquo;s logged as Defect 7, and it&rsquo;s the clearest case on the corrections page of a system that had what it needed to recover and didn&rsquo;t use it. (I wrote more about why a schedule can&rsquo;t stand in for this kind of follow-up in <a href="/blog/schedule-is-not-a-work-queue">the schedule is not a work queue</a>.)</p>

<p>Even when retry is the right move, the way you retry matters. The Amazon Builders&rsquo; Library has a good piece on <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/timeouts-retries-and-backoff-with-jitter/">timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter</a> that makes the point plainly: retries add load to a system that&rsquo;s already struggling, which is exactly when it can least afford it. That&rsquo;s why backoff (waiting longer between each attempt) and jitter (randomizing those waits so clients don&rsquo;t all come back at once) exist.</p>

<p>And retries multiply when they stack. Say your agent calls a tool, the tool calls a service, and the service calls a database, and each layer retries three times. That&rsquo;s four attempts per layer. One failing call at the top becomes 4 &times; 4 &times; 4 = 64 calls at the bottom layer. It wouldn&rsquo;t surprise me if a lot of agent stacks have the model framework retrying, the tool wrapper retrying, and the client library retrying, with nobody having written down that all three are on. So the retry decision belongs somewhere that can see the whole stack, which argues for the agent owning it.</p>

<h2>Did it already happen?</h2>

<p>This is the hard one. When a read times out, you can just read again; nothing changed. When a write times out, you don&rsquo;t know whether the write happened. The request may have reached the server, done its work, and then lost the response on the way back. Retry blindly and you might do it twice; give up and you might leave it half done.</p>

<p>The well-established answer here is idempotency: designing the action so that doing it twice has the same effect as doing it once. Stripe&rsquo;s documentation on <a href="https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests">idempotent requests</a> describes the pattern clearly. The client attaches a unique key to the request, and if the same key shows up again, the server returns the original result instead of charging the card a second time. That lets a client safely retry a request that may have already succeeded, which is precisely the unknown-outcome case.</p>

<p>But a lot of the APIs an agent calls don&rsquo;t offer idempotency keys. So the agent has to do the equivalent work itself: before re-running a side-effecting action, check whether the first attempt took effect. On 2026-10-09, this site&rsquo;s newsletter step called Mailchimp to create a campaign, and the call failed with a read error before any response came back. The system had no way of knowing from the error alone whether the campaign existed. So before retrying, it listed the existing campaigns and checked for one that matched. That&rsquo;s the unknown-outcome case handled correctly, and it&rsquo;s a small thing that a retry decorator cannot do, because a decorator doesn&rsquo;t know what &ldquo;already happened&rdquo; would look like for this particular action.</p>

<p>Later in the same run, the cycle polled the live URL after publishing. It got a 404 four times and a 200 (the web&rsquo;s &ldquo;OK&rdquo; response) on the fifth. That&rsquo;s a transient failure, and waiting was exactly right. Same run, two failures, two different correct responses. That&rsquo;s what classification buys you. (There&rsquo;s a related trap in trusting what a call says about itself, which I wrote about in <a href="/blog/success-code-is-a-self-report">a success code is a self-report</a>.)</p>

<h2>Where was I?</h2>

<p>Suppose you classified correctly, chose the right move, and made the retry safe. You still have to pick back up from the right place. If a ten-step workflow fails on step seven and the agent restarts from step one, it&rsquo;ll redo six steps that already happened, and some of those steps probably have side effects too. Now you&rsquo;re back in the unknown-outcome problem six times over.</p>

<p>Resuming correctly requires persisted state: a record, outside the running process, of which steps completed and what they produced. If the state only lives in the agent&rsquo;s context or in memory, it dies with the run. I&rsquo;ve argued before that this is <a href="/blog/state-store-problem">the state store problem</a>, and that a lot of what looks like agent unreliability is really amnesia. The September deferral I described above only works if the 3:05am run knows what the dead run already finished; otherwise it&rsquo;s a fresh start wearing a recovery costume. (The flip side, where the run died but the record survived and saved the day, is in <a href="/blog/the-run-died-the-entry-didnt">the run died, the entry didn&rsquo;t</a>.)</p>

<p>And there&rsquo;s a step that comes before all four, which is noticing. On 2026-09-08, a run on this site fired, wrote one commit, and then produced nothing. The only thing that noticed was a different task with no authority to fix it, and the slot sat empty for three days. That&rsquo;s Defect 6, and it&rsquo;s a reminder that recovery can&rsquo;t start without detection. The evaluation framework puts detection under Feedback Interpretation, and recovery leans on it entirely. You can&rsquo;t classify a failure you never saw.</p>

<h2>Isn&rsquo;t this just engineering?</h2>

<p>There&rsquo;s a fair counter-argument here, and I want to give it its full weight. Most real failures are transient. Rate limits clear, networks hiccup, deploys land. A retry with exponential backoff and jitter handles the large majority of what goes wrong in practice. And everything else I&rsquo;ve described (idempotency keys, durable state, deferred jobs) is ordinary distributed-systems engineering, built into payment systems and job queues long before anyone said &ldquo;agent.&rdquo; So why should it count as a test of agency at all?</p>

<p>The counter-argument is right about the frequencies; but frequency isn&rsquo;t the test. The disqualification says &ldquo;common failure states,&rdquo; plural, and a usage limit with a stated reset time is common. So is a write that times out. A system that handles the majority case and fumbles the rest isn&rsquo;t recovering; it&rsquo;s getting lucky most of the time, and the unlucky cases are exactly the ones that cost money or reputation. A duplicated payment is rare; but it&rsquo;s the one you&rsquo;ll be explaining to someone.</p>

<p>And yes, the mechanisms are ordinary engineering. That&rsquo;s fine. What makes it a question about agency is who chooses among them. In a traditional system, an engineer decides ahead of time that this endpoint gets three retries, that one gets an idempotency key, and this job runs again at midnight. The choice is frozen into code. An agent meets failures nobody anticipated, with error messages nobody parsed in advance, and it has to read the failure, decide what family it&rsquo;s in, and pick a move. The 3am reset time was sitting in plain English in the error message. No retry policy written beforehand would have known to look for it. An agent that reads it and schedules itself accordingly is doing something a decorator can&rsquo;t.</p>

<p>That&rsquo;s also why this matters on the <a href="/maturity">maturity scale</a>. Level 4, Conditional Agency, describes systems that lack &ldquo;robust rescoping, durable recovery, or generalized adaptive continuation.&rdquo; Level 5 is closed-loop goal pursuit without human handoffs, and every misclassified failure that ends in an email to a person is a human handoff. Defect 3 was one. Defect 7 became one by default, because the only thing that eventually moved was George. (The cost of those handoffs is the subject of <a href="/blog/human-handoff-problem">the human handoff problem</a>.)</p>

<h2>So what counts?</h2>

<p>Here&rsquo;s how I&rsquo;d read the disqualification in practice. A system recovers from common failure states if, for the failures it actually meets, it can tell the three families apart, choose among waiting, retrying, routing around, deferring, and escalating, check whether a side-effecting action already happened before repeating it, and resume from persisted state rather than from the top. Escalation is allowed; but it has to be chosen because the other moves don&rsquo;t fit, not reached for because the system didn&rsquo;t know what else to do.</p>

<p>The evaluation&rsquo;s pass logic is conjunctive, which means one failing domain fails the whole assessment. So a system with excellent planning and broad execution authority still doesn&rsquo;t pass if its recovery is a retry loop and a prayer. I think that&rsquo;s the right call. An agent that can&rsquo;t pick itself up correctly is an agent you have to watch, and an agent you have to watch hasn&rsquo;t taken anything off your plate.</p>

<p>So here&rsquo;s an exercise. Pick the last three failures your agent hit. For each one, write down which family it belonged to, which move the system made, and which move it should have made. Then pick the one side-effecting action in your workflow you&rsquo;d least like to see happen twice, and ask what your agent does if that call times out. If the honest answer is &ldquo;it tries again,&rdquo; you don&rsquo;t have recovery yet. You have a retry, and a retry is a guess.</p>

